6-06"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["06"]},"Don’t rely on vitamin C to prevent colds"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-07"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["07"]},"Don’t get a whole-body PET-CT or tumor marker panel if you have no symptoms"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-08"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["08"]},"Don’t expect fitness trackers or smartwatches to help you lose weight"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-09"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["09"]},"Don’t pay a premium for organic food just to be “healthier”"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-10"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["10"]},"Don’t spend a lot of money on health supplements, herbal formulas, or tonics to “improve your health”"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-11"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["11"]},"Don’t take probiotics long-term if you have no gut problems"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-12"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["12"]},"Don’t force yourself to take cold showers just to “boost immunity”"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-13"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["13"]},"Don’t buy products marketed for detox, colon cleansing, enzymes, or alkaline water"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-14"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["14"]},"Don’t force yourself to drink water just to hit “8 glasses a day”"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-15"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["15"]},"Don’t spend money on fortune‑telling, tarot, or astrology to make decisions"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-16"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["16"]},"Don’t buy blue-light-blocking glasses to “protect your vision,” and don’t believe claims that staring at screens for months will ruin your eyes — but severe eye pain, redness, and swelling require immediate medical attention"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-17"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["17"]},"Don’t treat blood donation as a health habit: claims that it “detoxifies, lowers cholesterol, and prevents heart attacks” lack randomized trial support, while frequent donation truly causes iron deficiency"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-18"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["18"]},"Don’t rely on generic health check packages to prevent disease; instead, get the evidence‑based screenings appropriate for your age and gender"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-19"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["19"]},"Don’t start taking uric‑acid‑lowering drugs just because a checkup shows high uric acid but you’ve never had symptoms"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"aria-current":"page","className":"active","href":"/en/tip/06-20","data-status":"active"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["20"]},"Don’t have a gallbladder removed prophylactically just because an ultrasound found gallstones but you’ve never felt any pain"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-21"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["21"]},"Don’t cut calcium out to prevent kidney stones"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-22"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["22"]},"Don’t buy crystals, bracelets, or Pixiu figurines for “good luck,” “wealth attraction,” or “health benefits”"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-23"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["23"]},"Don’t expect shopping to lift your mood or boost your sense of self-worth"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-24"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["24"]},"Don’t spend extra money to “move up a rung” among your peers by buying a new house, car, or social circle"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-25"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["25"]},"Don’t believe that “willpower is like a muscle that runs out after use”"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-26"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["26"]},"Don’t expect skipping breakfast or the 16:8 intermittent fasting method to help you control your weight; instead, pick a meal timing you can stick to long-term"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-27"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["27"]},"Do not believe that \"masturbation harms the body\" or that \"abstinence is the only way to improve\"; what truly matters is whether it interferes with your sleep, work, and relationships"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-28"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["28"]},"Do not spend money on \"conversion therapy\" or \"gay علاج,\" and do not send your family members to undergo it either"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-29"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["29"]},"Do not expect “correct lifting posture” or lifting training to prevent back pain; reduce the weight and number of lifts, and exercise regularly"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-30"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["30"]},"Do not expect extra calcium or bone broth to make a fracture heal faster"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/tip/06-31"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["31"]},"Don’t rely on an AI chatbot alone to judge a medical or legal problem; before seeing a doctor, going to court or spending big money, find the source it cites and check it against the original"]}]}]},{"tag":"div","props":{"className":"life-tip-page"},"children":[{"tag":"nav","props":{"className":"life-tip-breadcrumb","aria-label":"Breadcrumb"},"children":[{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/book"},"children":["Explore the book"]},{"tag":"span","props":{"aria-hidden":"true"},"children":["/"]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"/en/book/06"},"children":["Chapter 6"," ·"," ","Items to avoid"]}]},{"tag":"article","props":{"className":"life-entry-card status-unmarked is-standalone","id":"06-20"},"children":[{"tag":"div","props":{"className":"life-entry-top"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-entry-number"},"children":["20"]},{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-evidence grade-a"},"children":["Evidence"," ","A"]}]},{"tag":"h1","props":{"lang":"en"},"children":["Don’t have a gallbladder removed prophylactically just because an ultrasound found gallstones but you’ve never felt any pain"]},{"tag":"p","props":{"lang":"en","className":"life-entry-summary"},"children":["For people with gallstones but no pain, roughly 80% remained symptom-free over the next nine years. Without any intervention, only about 0.1% to 0.3% develop acute cholecystitis or pancreatitis each year. Gallbladder removal isn’t risk-free either: roughly 1.5% of patients suffer bile duct injury. That’s why European and UK medical guidelines recommend against surgery when there are no symptoms."]},{"tag":"div","props":{"className":"life-entry-impact"},"children":[{"tag":"div","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["Cost"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["There’s no cost at all — you even avoid the expense of gen…"]}]},{"tag":"div","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":["Benefit"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["In Italy, the MICOL study followed 11,229 people aged 29 to 69 using ultrasound screening. Of those, 856 had g…"]}]}]},{"tag":"div","props":{"className":"life-entry-actions","aria-label":"Mark: Don’t have a gallbladder removed prophylactically just because an ultrasound found gallstones but you’ve never felt any pain"},"children":[{"tag":"button","props":{"type":"button","className":"life-action done","aria-pressed":"false"},"children":[{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"16","height":"16","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-check","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M20 6 9 17l-5-5"},"children":[]}]},"Done"]},{"tag":"button","props":{"type":"button","className":"life-action todo","aria-pressed":"false"},"children":[{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"16","height":"16","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-list-todo","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"rect","props":{"x":"3","y":"5","width":"6","height":"6","rx":"1"},"children":[]},{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"m3 17 2 2 4-4"},"children":[]},{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M13 6h8"},"children":[]},{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M13 12h8"},"children":[]},{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M13 18h8"},"children":[]}]},"Plan to do"]},{"tag":"button","props":{"type":"button","className":"life-action favorite","aria-pressed":"false"},"children":[{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"16","height":"16","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-bookmark","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"m19 21-7-4-7 4V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h10a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v16z"},"children":[]}]},"Save"]}," ",{"tag":"button","props":{"type":"button","className":"social-share-trigger"},"children":[{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"16","height":"16","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-share2 lucide-share-2","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"circle","props":{"cx":"18","cy":"5","r":"3"},"children":[]},{"tag":"circle","props":{"cx":"6","cy":"12","r":"3"},"children":[]},{"tag":"circle","props":{"cx":"18","cy":"19","r":"3"},"children":[]},{"tag":"line","props":{"x1":"8.59","x2":"15.42","y1":"13.51","y2":"17.49"},"children":[]},{"tag":"line","props":{"x1":"15.41","x2":"8.59","y1":"6.51","y2":"10.49"},"children":[]}]},"Share this tip"]}]},{"tag":"button","props":{"type":"button","className":"life-detail-toggle","aria-expanded":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"10","height":"10","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"currentColor","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-circle","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"circle","props":{"cx":"12","cy":"12","r":"10"},"children":[]}]},"Hide details"]},{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"16","height":"16","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-chevron-down rotated","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"m6 9 6 6 6-6"},"children":[]}]}]},{"tag":"div","props":{"className":"life-entry-details"},"children":[{"tag":"div","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-detail-label"},"children":["Cost"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["There’s no cost at all — you even avoid the expense of general anesthesia and a hospital stay. The trade-off is simply remembering a few warning signs that mean you must seek medical care right away."]}]},{"tag":"div","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-detail-label"},"children":["Benefit"]},{"tag":"p","props":{},"children":["In Italy, the MICOL study followed 11,229 people aged 29 to 69 using ultrasound screening. Of those, 856 had gallstones; 580 had none at all. After an average of 8.7 years of follow-up, 453 of those 580 (78.1%) stayed symptom-free, 61 (10.5%) experienced mild symptoms, and 66 (11.4%) had severe symptoms. During that time, 189 gallbladder removals were performed, and 41.3% of those surgeries were on patients without any symptoms. The study authors concluded that watchful waiting remains the best approach for most patients. Another US study of 691 patients with gallstones showed that among 135 symptom-free patients followed for an average of 58 months, only 10% developed symptoms and 7% needed surgery. Of all 50 deaths recorded, just two were linked to the bile ducts, and both occurred in patients with symptoms. The authors stressed that people with silent gallstones shouldn’t undergo surgery before symptoms appear. UK NICE guidelines explicitly state that people with no symptoms and normal bile ducts don’t need treatment. European liver disease guidelines note that each year, 0.7% to 2.5% of symptom-free patients develop symptoms, while 0.1% to 0.3% suffer acute cholecystitis, pancreatitis, jaundice, or cholangitis. After the first episode of pain, that risk rises to 1% to 3% annually. Swedish national data show that 1.5% of 51,041 gallbladder removals cause bile duct injury, with 0.3% resulting in partial or total duct rupture; mortality within a year is 3.9% for those with injury versus 1.1% otherwise. Even after surgery, 10% to 40% of patients still experience discomfort. Overall, guidelines agree that surgery doesn’t extend life expectancy for symptom-free patients, and the procedure’s risks outweigh those of leaving the organ intact. Waiting until symptoms appear also saves money. One 1983 estimate found that a 30-year-old man who chooses prophylactic removal lives 4 days less on average, while a 50-year-old loses 18 days. To date, no randomized trials have compared prophylactic removal to watchful waiting, yet the idea that “it’s better to remove it now before pain starts” remains common."]}]},{"tag":"div","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-detail-label"},"children":["Original sources"]},{"tag":"p","props":{"className":"life-citations"},"children":["Festi D, Reggiani ML, Attili AF, et al. (2010). Natural history of gallstone disease: Expectant management or active treatment? Results from a population-based cohort study. Journal of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, 25(4), 719-724. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1440-1746.2009.06146.x","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1440-1746.2009.06146.x"]},"；McSherry CK, Ferstenberg H, Calhoun WF, Lahman E, Virshup M (1985). The natural history of diagnosed gallstone disease in symptomatic and asymptomatic patients. Annals of Surgery, 202(1), 59-63. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.1097/00000658-198507000-00009","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.1097/00000658-198507000-00009"]},"；National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (2014). Gallstone disease: diagnosis and management. NICE guideline CG188，第 1.2.1、1.3.1 条. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg188/chapter/Recommendations","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg188/chapter/Recommendations"]},"；European Association for the Study of the Liver (2016). EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the prevention, diagnosis and treatment of gallstones. Journal of Hepatology, 65(1), 146-181. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhep.2016.03.005","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhep.2016.03.005"]},"；Törnqvist B, Strömberg C, Persson G, Nilsson M (2012). Effect of intended intraoperative cholangiography and early detection of bile duct injury on survival after cholecystectomy: population based cohort study. BMJ, 345, e6457. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.e6457","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.e6457"]},"；Ransohoff DF, Gracie WA, Wolfenson LB, Neuhauser D (1983). Prophylactic cholecystectomy or expectant management for silent gallstones. A decision analysis to assess survival. Annals of Internal Medicine, 99(2), 199-204. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.7326/0003-4819-99-2-199","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.7326/0003-4819-99-2-199"]},"；Gurusamy KS, Samraj K (2007). Cholecystectomy versus no cholecystectomy in patients with silent gallstones. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, CD006230. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD006230.pub2","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD006230.pub2"]},"；反方：Venneman NG, Buskens E, Besselink MG, et al. (2005). Small gallstones are associated with increased risk of acute pancreatitis: potential benefits of prophylactic cholecystectomy? American Journal of Gastroenterology, 100(11), 2540-2550. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1572-0241.2005.00317.x","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1572-0241.2005.00317.x"]},"；中华医学会外科学分会胆道外科学组, 中国医师协会外科医师分会胆道外科医师委员会 (2022). 胆囊良性疾病外科治疗的专家共识（2021版）. 中华外科杂志, 60(1), 4-9. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.3760/cma.j.cn112139-20210811-00373","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.3760/cma.j.cn112139-20210811-00373"]},"（全文需订阅，未取得）；同上 (2022). 《胆囊良性疾病外科治疗的专家共识（2021版）》解读. 中华外科杂志, 60(4), 337-342. ",{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.3760/cma.j.cn112139-20220119-00031","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"children":["https://doi.org/10.3760/cma.j.cn112139-20220119-00031"]}]},{"tag":"a","props":{"href":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1440-1746.2009.06146.x","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"life-source-link"},"children":["Open source link"," ",{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"13","height":"13","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-external-link","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M15 3h6v6"},"children":[]},{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M10 14 21 3"},"children":[]},{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M18 13v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"},"children":[]}]}]}]},{"tag":"div","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-detail-label"},"children":["Book note"]},{"tag":"p","props":{"className":"life-citations"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-content-reference"},"children":["This topic is still debated. China’s 2021 expert consensus on biliary surgery includes some asymptomatic gallstone cases as surgical candidates, and related commentary highlights this controversy as a key point of discussion. The exact criteria for those exceptions haven’t been verified yet. Another counterpoint concerns small stones: those under 5 mm are more closely linked to pancreatitis, and Dutch research suggests prophylactic removal might either extend or shorten life depending on how common and severe pancreatitis is in a given population. European guidelines do list two exceptions: porcelain gallbladder and gallstones over 1 cm alongside large polyps — both warrant removal. Removal may also be considered during other major abdominal surgeries. Crucially, this advice applies only to stones inside the gallbladder with no prior symptoms; stones in the common bile duct are a completely different matter. UK and European guidelines both stress that stones in the bile duct must be removed along with the gallbladder, regardless of symptoms. Seek emergency care immediately if you experience persistent upper abdominal pain, fever, chills, yellowing of the eyes or skin, or dark urine. When a doctor recommends surgery after reviewing imaging, ask which specific criteria prompted that decision — whether it’s bile duct involvement, polyps, or abnormal gallbladder walls — so you can weigh those factors against this general guideline, which is meant to discourage surgery without any valid medical reason. Both cited studies tracked patients over 4 to 9 years, focusing on what happens when no intervention is taken; they don’t compare treatment effectiveness. Longer follow-ups would likely show a higher rate of symptom development. 〔61.6〕 〔50.2〕 〔46.3〕 〔188〕 〔2016〕"]}]}]},{"tag":"div","props":{"className":"life-personal-note"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{"className":"life-detail-label"},"children":["My note"]},{"tag":"button","props":{"type":"button"},"children":["Write a reminder for yourself…"]}]}]}]},{"tag":"nav","props":{"className":"life-tip-pager"},"children":[{"tag":"a","props":{"className":"life-tip-pager-link","href":"/en/tip/06-19"},"children":[{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"16","height":"16","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-left","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"m12 19-7-7 7-7"},"children":[]},{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M19 12H5"},"children":[]}]},{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"small","props":{},"children":["Previous"]},"Don’t start taking uric‑acid‑lowering drugs just because a checkup shows high uric acid but you’ve never had symptoms"]}]},{"tag":"a","props":{"className":"life-tip-pager-link next","href":"/en/tip/06-21"},"children":[{"tag":"span","props":{},"children":[{"tag":"small","props":{},"children":["Next"]},"Don’t cut calcium out to prevent kidney stones"]},{"tag":"svg","props":{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":"16","height":"16","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right","aria-hidden":"true"},"children":[{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"M5 12h14"},"children":[]},{"tag":"path","props":{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"},"children":[]}]}]}]},{"tag":"sect